"""
The first board's last layer, with a light source switched on.

pta_board_chain.py, pta_board_range.py and pta_board_bits.py ran a stand-in
for the GRXGPU charter's first-board classifier with its last layer on the
tile, and every one of their runs had the tile's own noise and no source:
grx930's harness would not light a tile that had only some of a network's
layers.  It now does.  This is that layer with the source as this plan holds
it (B16):

    shared     its noise within 1% rms a shot for the lines together
    a line     and within 5% for a line on its own
    level      its lines level to 20% as they reach the tile, read with the
               level probe at sixteen shots a row and corrected on the
               weights, a row at a time, written at 8 bits

on the 8-bit converter of B14, with the range training's rule gives and with
the range fitted to the reference.

HOW THE LIGHT IS MODELLED, which is grx930's harness's way and is first
order.  A shot's sum gains, for each row, its product times its line's error,
and that share is added after the converter: it is neither rounded nor held
at the range.  A correction on the weights rewrites them before the tile, so
the converter sees the sums of the rewritten weights, and the light's share
then brings them back.  A real converter sees the two together.  So where a
row's weights were raised for a dim line the model's converter sees a sum
larger than a chip's would, and can hold it at the range where a chip would
not: the sums past the range in a row whose lines are read are the model's
order as much as a source's.

MEASURED IN A MODEL, by grx930 (c930/doc/pta_error_model_design_note.md section
5, "The first board's last layer, with a light source switched on",
2026-10-13; `SHAPE=chain sim/pta_mnist.sh DIR WORK chainlit`, and `pta_mnist
eval --host` with a source).  pta_board_chain.py's networks, tile and draws,
ten rows: its format alone and its tile as budgeted, which are that file's
runs, line for line; the tile as budgeted with its range fitted, which is
pta_board_range.py's; the tile as budgeted with each of the source's rows
alone, the lines' level twice, left alone and read; with all three, which is
the source; that with the range fitted; and the format alone with the source.
On 10,000 images and on the first thousand.  A lit row is read over its own
row with no source, at the same range and of its draw.

DERIVED here:

  a row's mean and  which have to be the eight tables grx930 printed, cell
  its error         for cell.  A row with no source is worked out from
                    pta_board_chain.py's and pta_board_range.py's figures,
                    which this file does not hold again
  what a row adds   how often right in the row it is read over, less in the
                    row, network by network
  a row's share     what it adds to its row with no source, squares for
                    squares: the root of its rms squared less the other's,
                    run for run and then a mean.  It is what the row would
                    add if its error and the tile's were independent
  what a source     a network's rms error with it over its rms error with
  costs             none, draw for draw

HOW SURE.  Five networks, 2.8 errors for one in twenty.  A lit run with no
weight rewritten has its unlit run's converter, sum for sum, so what it adds
is the light's share and nothing else.  A run whose lines are read has other
weights on the tile, and is another run.

WHAT THIS IS NOT.  grxgpu's classifier, or a chip: everything
pta_board_chain.py says of itself.  A converter that sees the light: above.
A source through an offset, a cycle of calibrations, or a drifted tile: this
is the source over the tile as budgeted, as written.  A remedy.

Standard library only.  Run:  python3 docs/designs/pta_board_lit.py
"""
